  \end{align*}
Since $\mathop{\text{lcm}}$ and $\gcd$ are multiplicative, and both are defined on a finite set, $\gcd(m,10^{10})$ is a factor of $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$. Therefore, the only possible values for $\gcd(m,10^{10})$ are factors of 10: 1, 2, 5, or 10. If $\gcd(m,10^{10})=1$, that would mean $m$ is prime and 401 is not a multiple of a positive 3-digit integer, hence not possible.

If $\gcd(m,10^{10})=2$, taking the 10th powers:
\begin{align*}
