\implies &\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]^{10} = 5^{10}\cdot10^{100} \quad \text{Equation (1)}
\end{align*}
This equation gives us the form of $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$. Now, since $\gcd(m,10^{10})=2$, so the prime factorization of $m$ will have some power of 2 and some prime factor that is possible for $m$: 3, 5, or 7. And, there should be 9 other integers between $m$ and its multiples by 2, whose $\mathop{\text{lcm}}$ is known from equation (1), that are less than $10^3\cdot 10^{10}$. As you can verify by the Definition of Least Common Multiple, this set of possibilities satisfies the given properties.

