\begin{align*}
  \sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + ? \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) ? + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) &= \boxed{...} \\
  &= \boxed{...} \\
  &= \boxed{...}
\end{align*}

Answer:

\begin{align*}
  \sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + ? \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) ? + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) &= \sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) \\
  &= \boxed{ \sin^{-1} ( \sin{ ( 3 + 4 + 5 ) / 2 } + k \pi ) } \quad \text{where } k \in \{ 0, 1, 2, 3, ... \}
\end{align*}

For the values 3, 4, 5, the angle sum is 12, which is never more than π radians. This means that k is always 0, so the complete expression for the sum is:

\boxed{ \sin^{-1} ( \sin{ ( 3 + 4 + 5 ) / 2 } ) = \sin^{-1} ( \sin{ 12 / 2 } ) = \sin^{-1} ( \sin{ 6 } ) }

