Following your instructions for writing a LaTeX problem with the step-by-step solution, I've provided the steps for finding the number of prime factors in the product of two numbers. The solution also calculates the count of a common prime factor and then combines the counts of unique prime factors. Finally, I've wrapped the final answer in a \boxed{} command as requested.

Here's the full code:

```
\documentclass[12pt]{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\ProvideCommand{\prime}{'}
\newcommand{\PrimeFactorCount}[1] {#1 - 1}

\begin{document}

\subsection*{How many different prime factors are in $117 \cdot 119$, the prime factorization of $117 \cdot 119$}

\begin{enumerate}

    \item Find the prime factorization of each number.

    \begin{align*}
        117 &= 7 \cdot 17, \\
        119 &= 7 \cdot 17 \cdot \text{a prime}, \\
    \end{align*}
    where 7 is the common prime factor.

    \item Calculate the count of unique prime factors for each number.

    \begin{align*}
        \text{Count of factors for 117} &= \PrimeFactorCount{7} + \PrimeFactorCount{17} - \boxed{\PrimeFactorCount{7}} = 1 + 2 - 1 = 2, \\
        \text{Count of factors for 119} &= \PrimeFactorCount{7} + \PrimeFactorCount{17} + \PrimeFactorCount{\text{a prime}} - \boxed{PrimeFactorCount{7}} = 1 + 2 + \text{a prime} - 1 = 2 + \text{a prime}, \\
    \end{align*}

    \item Combine the counts of prime factors for the two numbers.

    \begin{align*}
        \text{Total count of prime factors} &= \boxed{2 + 2} = \boxed{4 - 2} = \boxed{\PrimeFactorCount{2}} = \boxed{2 \text{ (since 2 is indeed a prime number)}}, \\
    \end{align*}

\end{enumerate}

\end{document}
```

