\Large
How many different prime factors are in $(117\cdot 119)$? \\

To solve this problem, we will find the prime factorization of $117$ and $119$.\\

\textbf{Step 1: Prime Factorization of 117}.\\
\begin{align*}
117 &= 7\times 17 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 117 = 7^1 \timess 17^1 \\
\text{Since }7\text{ and }17\text{ are both prime numbers, their exponent are their multiplicity in the prime factorization of }117.
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2: Prime Factorization of 119}.\\
\begin{align*}
119 &= 7\times 17 \mul 3
 \quad \Rightarrow \quad
119 &= 7^1 \timess 17^1 \timess 3^1 \\
\text{Since }3\text{ is a prime number, its exponent is equal to its multiplicity in the prime factorization of }119.
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: The Primary Factors}
Now we have all numbers that occur as a factor in $117\cdot 119$ and here they with their multiplicity (number of times each number occurs): \\

$\boxed{\begin{tabu}{|c|c|c|}\hline
      \text{Primary Factor } & \text{Multiplicity} &  \text{Exponent} \\\hline
      3                  &          1           &        1 \\\hline
      7                  &          2           &        1 \\\hline
      17                 &          2           &        1 \\\hline\end{tabu}}$

\textbf{Step 4: The Different Prime Factors} \\
We count only the prime numbers and ignore the multiplicity, so we have: \\

$\boxed{\begin{tabu}{|c|c|c|}\hline
      \text{Different Prime Factors } & \text{ } \\\hline
      3                  &           \\\hline
      7                  &           \\\hline
      17                 &           \\\hline\end{tabu}}$

As we can see there are three different prime factors in $(117\cdot 119)$.
\end{center}
\end{document}
