Here is a step-by-step solution to the problem:

First, we'll subtract the terms with $4$ in the exponent:

\begin{align*}
  & 2009^4 - 4 \times 2007^4 + 6 \times 2005^4 - 4 \times 2003^4 \\
  =& 2009^4 - 4 \times 2007^4 + 6 \times 2005^4 - 4 \times 2003^4 + 4 \times (2007^4 - 2003^4) \\
  =& (2009^4 + 6 \times 2005^4) + 4 \times (2007^4 - 2003^4) \\
\end{align*}

Next, we'll add and subtract the terms with $5$ and $3$ in the exponent in a clever way:

\begin{align*}
  =& (2009^4 + 6 \times 2005^4) + 4 \times (2007^4 - 2003^4) \\
  =& (2009^4 + 6 \times 2005^4) + 4 \times (2007^4 - 2007^4 + 2005^4 - 2003^4) \\
  =& (2009^4 + 6 \times 2005^4) + 4 \times 2005^4 - 4 \times 2003^4 \\
\end{align*}

Finally, since we added $6 \times 2005^4$ twice, we can remove the duplicated term:

\begin{align*}
  &= (2009^4 + 6 \times 2005^4) + 4 \times 2005^4 - 4 \times 2003^4 \\
  &= (2009^4 + 2 \times 6 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times 2003^4 \\
\end{align*}

Now, let's apply the difference of cubes formula:

\begin{align*}
  &= (2009^4 + 2 \times 6 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) \\
  &= (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) \\
  &= (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4 + 2003^3 \times 2001^3) \\
  &= (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) - 4 \times 2003^3 \times 2001^3 \\
\end{align*}

Combine the remaining terms:

\begin{align*}
  &= (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) - 4 \times 2003^3 \times 2001^3 \\
  &= (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) - (2003^3 \times 2001^3)^4 \\
\end{align*}

Now, we can rearrange the terms and take the cube of $(2003 \times 2001)^3$:

\begin{align*}
  =& (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) - (2003 \times 2001)^3 \times (2003 \times 2001)^3 \times (2003 \times 2001)^3 \\
  =& (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) - (2003^3 \times 2001^3)^4 \\
  =& (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) - (2003^3 \times 2001^3)^4 \\
  =& (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) \\
  &\qquad - [(2003 \times 2001)^3]^4 \\
\end{align*}

Plug in the values:

\begin{align*}
  =& (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) \\
  &\qquad - [(6 \times 1003)^3]^4 \\
  =& (2009^4 + 12 \times 2005^4) - 4 \times (2003^4 + 2001^4) \\
  &\qquad - (7.2 \times 10^6)^4 \\
\end{align*}

Calculate the exact values:

\begin{align*}
  =& (1.008 \times 10^{16}) - 4 \times (3.281 \times 10^{12}) - (7.2 \times 10^6)^4 \\
  &= 1.008 \times 10^{16} - 4 \times 3.281 \times 10^{12} - (7.2 \times 10^6)^4 \\
\end{align*}

Square the last term:

\begin{align*}
  &= 1.008 \times 10^{16} - 4 \times 3.281 \times 10^{12} - (7.2 \times 10^6)^4 \\
  &= 1.008 \times 10^{16} - 4 \times 3.281 \times 10^{12} - (5.187 \times 10^{12})^2 \\
\end{align*}

\boxed{ 1.008 \times 10^{16} - 4 \times 3.281 \times 10^{12} - (5.187 \times 10^{12})^2 }

You may write the final answer in a standard form as follows:

\boxed{ 1.008 \text{ quadrillion } - 4 \times 3.281 \text{ billion } - (5.187 \times 10^{12})^2 }

